    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d716">

                <pb facs="0822" n="812" sameAs="frus:frus1948v07/pg812"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00B/4–648: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in China</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Stuart</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1948-04-30T19:00:00-04:00">April 30, 1948—7
                            p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>652. Does Emb think reconsideration of question retaining consular offices in
                    cities threatened by Communist occupation (particularly <pb facs="0823" n="813"
                        xml:id="pg813"/> Mukden) warranted in light possibility increased anti-US
                    feeling arising from China aid program as reported Shanghai’s tel. 933 Apr
                        27<anchor n="11" xml:id="d716-f1"/> and Communist attitude toward foreigners
                    reported Mukden’s tel. 198 Mar 30<anchor n="12" xml:id="d716-f2"/> to you?</p>
                <p>Although Dept aware Communist attitude toward retention consular offices
                    described in Hong Kong’s memo Mar 9<anchor n="13" xml:id="d716-f3"/> forwarded
                    with Embdes 164 Apr 6<anchor n="14" xml:id="d716-f4"/> may well differ from that
                    of Communist leaders, it believes contents this memo would be of interest to
                    Mukden and possibly other consular offices north China, If Emb has not already
                    done so, it should communicate pertinent portions this memo to those
                    offices.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Marshall</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
